sequence the work, not re-explain each one.

OPEN ITEMS (already known to this person; do not restate what each one is)
{% for finding in findings %}
- id: {{ finding.indicator_id }} | {{ finding.title }} | current state: {{ finding.severity_label }}
{% endfor %}

NATURAL ORDER (a guide, not a rigid template — skip any category with no
open item in it, and never invent a step for something not listed above)
1. Naming — agreeing what each field is called and what it means, and
   keeping each field to one fact
2. Shared vocabulary — a common data dictionary and standardized category
   values every site actually uses
3. Linking and mapping — a way to match records across sites without
   exposing who anyone is, and a written record of how each site's own
   fields map onto the shared structure

An item marked "not started" still gets a real step — a genuinely useful
first move toward beginning it, not a placeholder. Frame that step's DETAIL
as a starting point, not a finished fix.

YOUR TASK
Group the open items above into 2-6 ordered steps following that natural
order (merge multiple items into one step when they belong to the same
category; skip categories with nothing open). Every item listed above must
be addressed by exactly one step — do not drop any, do not invent items not
listed.

OUTPUT FORMAT — follow this exactly, no other text before or after:

GOAL: <one plain sentence: what doing all of this achieves and why it
matters for {{ subject_label }}>
STEP: <short imperative title, e.g. "Agree on one shared field name per concept">
ADDRESSES: <comma-separated indicator ids from the list above that this step covers>
DETAIL: <1-2 plain sentences on what to actually do, grounded in the items this
step addresses>
STEP: <next step>
ADDRESSES: <ids>
DETAIL: <...>

RULES
- Never use an acronym or technical term without explaining it in plain
  words the moment you first use it. Assume zero prior data-harmonization
  knowledge.
- Do not mention "FAIR-DSM," "data maturity levels," indicator codes, or
  RDA/FAIRplus jargon anywhere.
- ADDRESSES must only ever contain ids copied exactly from the OPEN ITEMS
  list above — never invent, abbreviate, or guess an id.
- Plain language, second person ("you"). GOAL and each DETAIL: max 40 words.
